---
// Slot order: Claim · Evidence · Explore · Limits · Cite. Method and data live on /data/.
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import Claim from '../../../components/Claim.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import ExploreDoor from '../../../components/ExploreDoor.astro';
import Chronology from '../../../components/figures/Chronology.astro';
import DirectionChart from '../../../components/figures/DirectionChart.astro';
import MechanismChart from '../../../components/figures/MechanismChart.astro';
import Residents from '../../../components/figures/Residents.astro';
import core from '../../../data/core.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';
import { personHref } from '../../../data/bb';

const c = core.counts;
const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'buddhist-bridges')!;
const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Buddhist Bridges: India–Korea Buddhist Mediators Dataset (v3.0)',
  description: 'Source-graded dataset of documented mediators of India–Korea Buddhist exchange, 300–2026 CE.',
  creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mohd Bilal' },
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
  version: '3.0',
  datePublished: '2026-08-17',
};
---
<Project title="Five bridges, one silence"
  description="Who mediated Buddhist exchange between India and Korea, 300–2026? A source-graded prosopography of 43 documented mediators: five short-lived bridges and one five-hundred-year silence."
  citationTitle="Five Bridges, One Silence: Documented Mediators of India–Korea Buddhist Exchange, 300–2026"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
  <div class="wrap">

    <Claim project={p}
      meta={`${c.people_carried} documented mediators · ${c.events} events · ${c.sources} graded sources · every claim opens to its record and its sources`} />

    <Chronology />

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Evidence I</span>The direction inverts</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Before 900 the bridge was made of journeys, and it ran both ways. Of eighteen carried mediators,
          eight moved toward India and eight carried Buddhism toward Korea. No later era is balanced. From
          Koryŏ onward India comes to Korea: first in one person, the monk Dhyānabhadra, then in texts, then
          in institutions. Two-way movement does not return until the 1990s.
        </p>
      </div>
      <DirectionChart />
      <Apparatus label="The evidence, close up">
        <p>
          The best documented life of the balanced era is Hyech'o (<span lang="ko">혜초, 慧超</span>, 704–787),
          who sailed to India around 719 and walked back through Kashmir, Persia and Central Asia. His
          <i>Wang ochŏnch'ukkuk chŏn</i>, the only surviving eighth-century East Asian account of India, spent
          a millennium in the Dunhuang cave library and was first exhibited in Korea in 2010.
          <a href={personHref('P-0001')}>Open his record →</a>
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Evidence II</span>Five hundred years of silence</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The most important number in the dataset is zero. For the whole of Chosŏn (1392–1900) no person,
          journey, text or institution meets the evidence bar. Eleven candidates were examined and all were
          excluded, as the Neo-Confucian state closed the channels Buddhism had used. Between the enshrinement
          of Dhyānabhadra's relics in 1370 and the next documented event, a delegation of 1981, lie 611 years.
          A narrative of unbroken transmission has to pass over these five centuries in silence; the record
          does not support it. What survived travelled by lineage and by print until a later generation could
          rebuild the crossing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Apparatus label="What a zero does and does not mean">
        <p>
          The zero is a finding under stated inclusion criteria, and it is documented: the eleven excluded
          candidates remain in the dataset for inspection. It is not a claim that no contact ever occurred.
          Following the practice of the Prosopography of the Byzantine World, it should never be assumed that
          what this dataset does not contain does not exist.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Evidence III</span>The bridge changes material</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each rebuilding used a different substance. The ancient bridge was made of people who travelled. The
          colonial-era bridge was made of paper: four of its six mediators were not Buddhists, both attempted
          pilgrimages went unrealised, and what crossed was text, above all Tagore in Korean translation. The
          present bridge is institutional, carried by orders, universities and pilgrimage programmes; movement
          has returned, and the record includes its first women.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MechanismChart />
      <Apparatus label="The evidence, close up">
        <p>
          Two documents anchor the colonial chapter: the six-line message Tagore sent the <i>Tonga ilbo</i> in
          1929, remembered as “The Lamp of the East,” and the fifteen-line poem later circulated under the same
          title, which the dataset records as a composite fabrication. One event closes the longer arc: in 2024
          relics from Hoeamsa, where Dhyānabhadra's remains were enshrined in 1370, returned to Korea from the
          Boston Museum of Fine Arts. <a href={personHref('P-0030')}>Tagore's record →</a>
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>One role recurs in every era of contact and in no other: the mediator who stays.</p>
      </div>
      <Residents />
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Why it matters</span>A periodisation, and a case for small datasets</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The relationship is usually told either as myth or as modern diplomacy, and the documented record
          supports neither. A connection built five times out of changing materials, and interrupted for half a
          millennium, periodises the relationship, gives the Chosŏn interruption evidential weight, and shows
          transmission surviving without contact. It is also a case for small, carefully graded datasets:
          {c.people_carried} people with full sourcing can carry an argument a large scraped corpus cannot.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Explore</span>The evidence itself</h2>
      <ExploreDoor href={p.explore!.href} label={p.explore!.label} blurb={p.explore!.blurb} />
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Limits</span>What this project does not claim</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul class="limits">
          <li><b>All analysis is descriptive.</b> n = {c.people_carried}; no inferential claim is made from these counts.</li>
          <li><b>The modern era is documented mainly through the press,</b> which is a different evidentiary regime from the premodern chapters and is not directly comparable to them.</li>
          <li><b>Korean-language sources dominate.</b> An Indian-source corroboration round has not been carried out.</li>
          <li><b>No woman appears in the record before 1990.</b> The register records that absence; it cannot by itself explain it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">
          Inclusion criteria, confidence grading, downloads, licence and citation are on the
          <a href="/projects/buddhist-bridges/data/">data page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Project>
<style>
  .limits { max-width: var(--w-measure); }
  .limits li { margin-bottom: 10px; }
</style>
